(a) An employee in the classified service is entitled to request a hearing before a hearing officer when the employee has been the subject of disciplinary action resulting in the employee's suspension, demotion, or discharge. The issues that may be heard and determined by the hearing officer are:
(1) Whether the employee is guilty of the charges that are the basis for the disciplinary action; and
(2) Whether the disciplinary action imposed by an appointing authority is appropriate.
(b) An employee in the classified service is not entitled to request a hearing for reasons which include, but are not limited to, the following:
(1) Issues which are pending before or have been concluded by other administrative or judicial bodies;
(2) Work assignments which do not result in a demotion as defined in section 7-84;
(3) Budget allocations or changes in organizational structure, including decisions concerning the persons or number of persons assigned to particular jobs or units;
(4) The content or rating of a performance appraisal;
(5) The appointing authority's decision to select or not to select an individual to fill a position through appointment, promotion, or transfer;
(6) Any matter which is not within the jurisdiction or control of the appointing authority; or
(7) Internal security practices established by the appointing authority.
(1994 Ga. Laws (Act No. 1068), page 4686, § 1(pt. 19A); 1997 Ga. Laws (Act No. 239), page 4099, § 8)
Editor's note— The 1994 act added the above section as pt. 19A of 1955 Ga. Laws (Act No. 74), page 682.

